      "writtenOn": "2025-07-18T14:15:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually now I see at least one use for it: it allows the host to clear pvmfw as early as possible, i.e. even before reprivileging CPUs. Which indeed seems useful: something might go wrong during reprivileging, so the CPU e.g. gets stuck and doesn\u0027t end up clearing pvmfw, so it\u0027s better to ensure clearing it before that.\n\nBut, thinking about it further: pKVM can clear pvmfw here on its own, it doesn\u0027t need to delegate this task to the host? (And that would be a good safety measure in any case?)",
      "parentUuid": "7f838e18_c5a9606a",
      "revId": "08f1b0eeab64a890f0819adf3a3b153513fe0a4f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d08f6283_460b0fd8",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/init_finalise.c",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 341,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-18T14:47:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Per my latest comment in https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/3590992/comment/9b2cd540_fc70f3db/ (that it doesn\u0027t make much difference whether to clear pvmfw here in the hypercall, or in the host right after returning from this hypercall, so it might be better to do it in the host in all cases, for simplicity), I agree it\u0027s useful to unprotect the hypervisor memory (or at least just the pvmfw memory) here.\n\nStill, I\u0027m just curious: any other reasons to unprotect it here, besides clearing pvmfw?",
      "parentUuid": "42807ae5_815e7e30",
      "revId": "08f1b0eeab64a890f0819adf3a3b153513fe0a4f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
